<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-5.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-5.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="416" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112434" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-5.jpeg 896w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-5-300x149.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-5-768x381.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>A <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/04/17/perplexingly-book-learned-emacs/">commenter on my previous post about this stuff</a> suggested using <a href="https://www.librarything.com/developer/documentation/thingapis" data-cached-time="2025-04-22T13:34:33" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/cache-2025-04-22-web.webp" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">LibraryThing</a> to deduplicate editions, so I thought I&#8217;d give it a go.  I&#8217;m using Amy Hempel as the test case, because she&#8217;s only published a handful of books.</p>
<p>Or as <a href="https://openlibrary.org/dev/docs/api/search" data-cached-time="2025-04-22T13:34:44" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/cache-2025-04-22-web-1-scaled.webp" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">OpenLibrary</a> says: 27.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="161" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112421" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22.jpeg 981w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-300x58.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-768x148.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Let&#8217;s have a look at, say, the collection from 2006:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="879" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112422" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-1.jpeg 917w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-1-287x300.jpeg 287w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-1-768x804.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>The documentation says that it&#8217;s supposed to return a list of &#8220;works&#8221;, not editions, but of course the data here doesn&#8217;t have much quality control.  So here we have <tt>"The Collected Stories of Amy Hempel"</tt>, <tt>"The collected stories of Amy Hempel"</tt>, and finally <tt>"Collected Stories of Amy Hempel"</tt>.  For these, we have in total three different ISBNs (ISBN-10 and ISBN-13 are both listed in the output, apparently).</p>
<p>Let&#8217;s look up one of these in the LibraryThing API:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-2.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-2.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="54" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112423" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-2.jpeg 1350w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-2-300x20.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-2-1024x67.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-2-768x50.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>And actually&#8230;  it looks like LibraryThing does ISBN-10 only?  But it kinda looks like the LibraryThing de-duplication would work for that book.  So that&#8217;s promising, even if it means doing a whole bunch of calls to LibraryThing.</p>
<p>Hm&#8230;  Oh, OpenLibrary also lists this:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-3.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="189" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112424" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-3.jpeg 1164w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-3-300x68.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-3-1024x231.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-3-768x174.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Which is a translated edition, but would also have gotten caught by the LibraryThing de-duplication.  OK, I think I&#8217;m going to code up something and see what I get.</p>
<p><em>type type type</em></p>
<p>Viola!</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-4.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-4.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="406" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112432" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-4.jpeg 913w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-4-300x145.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-22-4-768x372.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>That&#8217;s actually a pretty good list!  OpenLibrary returned 27 publications, and after 17 LibraryThing API calls, we&#8217;re down to 12 works.</p>
<p>(There&#8217;s only five-ish of these that are &#8220;books by Amy Hempel&#8221; by any reasonable measure, but the rest are chapbooks, collections and collaborations, so it&#8217;s OK that they&#8217;re listed.)</p>
<p>Now, the number of LibraryThing API calls would make it pretty abusive to use this on a more prolific author, but as a proof of concept, it works.</p>
<p>(LibraryThing publishes data dumps of all this stuff, which would be more sensible to use, but that apparently costs $$$.)</p>
<p>So&#8230;  uhm&#8230;  could I use this to get &#8220;give all books published by the fifty authors I follow published since 2023&#8221;?  I think that would be possible: For each author, ask OpenLibrary for the list, and then for each book published after 2023, do the LibraryThing deduplication to see whether it&#8217;s a book that also appears earlier on the OpenLibrary list?  Yeah, I think that could work, but I guess the proof is in the programming.</p>
<p>Anyway!  Since I&#8217;m blathering on about this here, amusingly enough the previous post landed on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=43735896" data-cached-time="2025-04-22T14:58:49" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/cache-2025-04-22-web-2.webp" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">&#8220;Hacker News&#8221;</a>.  But it didn&#8217;t get enough points to get high enough on their home to totally ruin my statistics chart:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-21-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-21-3.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="455" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112356" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-21-3.jpeg 939w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-21-3-300x163.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-21-3-768x416.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
